  "full_response": "When you type a URL (e.g., `https://www.example.com`) into your browser and press **Enter**, a multi-step **DNS (Domain Name System) resolution** process occurs to translate the human-readable domain name into an **IP address** that computers use to communicate. Here’s a step-by-step breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Browser Cache Check**\n- The browser first checks its **local cache** to see if it already has the IP address for the domain.\n- If found, the process skips to step 8 (connecting to the server).\n\n---\n\n### **2. OS Cache Check**\n- If the browser doesn’t have the IP cached, it asks the **operating system (OS)** (e.g., Windows, macOS, Linux) to check its DNS cache.\n- The OS may have stored the IP from a recent request.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Router Cache Check**\n- If the OS doesn’t have the IP, the request is sent to the **local router**, which may have its own DNS cache.\n\n---\n\n### **4. ISP Recursive Resolver Check**\n- If the router doesn’t have the IP, the request is forwarded to the **ISP’s (Internet Service Provider) recursive DNS resolver** (e.g., Google’s `8.8.8.8`, Cloudflare’s `1.1.1.1`).\n- The resolver checks its cache and returns the IP if available.\n\n---\n\n### **5. Root DNS Servers (If Not Cached)**\n- If the ISP’s resolver doesn’t have the IP cached, it starts a **recursive DNS query** by contacting the **root DNS servers** (e.g., `.`).\n- The root servers don’t know the IP but direct the resolver to the **Top-Level Domain (TLD) servers** (e.g., `.com`, `.org`).\n\n---\n\n### **6. TLD DNS Servers**\n- The resolver queries the **TLD servers** (e.g., `.com` servers for `example.com`).\n- The TLD servers don’t know the exact IP but direct the resolver to the **authoritative DNS servers** for the domain.\n\n---\n\n### **7. Authoritative DNS Servers**\n- The resolver queries the **authoritative DNS servers** (managed by the domain’s registrar or hosting provider, e.g., Cloudflare, AWS Route 53).\n- These servers return the **IP address** (e.g., `93.184.21",
